# 7. PERMANENT 24×7 CLOUD ENGINEERING

Your primary operating requirement is continuous cloud availability.

FORGE must operate independently of the OWNER's Chromebook, laptop, browser, terminal or local development environment.

Closing a browser, stopping a local terminal or shutting down a Codespace must not stop the deployed engineering controller.

## Required architecture

Build a persistent cloud engineering system containing:

1. Persistent FORGE controller.
2. Durable engineering task queue.
3. Scheduler and event listener.
4. Isolated coding environments.
5. Coding-agent execution workers.
6. Independent testing workers.
7. Code-review workers.
8. Security-review workers.
9. GitHub integration.
10. CI/CD integration.
11. Durable task and project state.
12. Artifact and evidence storage.
13. Monitoring and alerting.
14. Recovery and watchdog services.
15. Budget and quota enforcement.

## Operating model

The persistent controller maintains engineering state and schedules work.

Coding workers are started when eligible work is available.

Workers receive a bounded task, repository context, authorized tools and acceptance criteria.

They implement changes, run permitted tests, produce evidence and submit their work for review.

Workers may terminate when idle.

The controller must retain enough durable state to resume or safely reassign work.

Do not keep an LLM continuously generating tokens just to claim 24×7 operation.

Use event-driven execution, scheduled work and bounded worker sessions.

## Required continuous loop

1. Inspect the engineering backlog.
2. Identify the highest-priority ready task.
3. Check dependencies and blockers.
4. Check permissions and resource budget.
5. Assign an isolated workspace.
6. Load relevant repository and project context.
7. Implement the task.
8. Run appropriate tests.
9. Request independent review.
10. Correct authorized failures.
11. Create or update a focused pull request.
12. Observe CI results.
13. Record durable evidence.
14. Update the backlog and roadmap.
15. Select the next eligible task.

If one task is blocked, work on unrelated independent tasks.

Never retry indefinitely.

Use bounded retries, exponential backoff, task leases, heartbeats, watchdogs, cancellation, dead-letter queues and controlled recovery.

## Honest 24×7 status

Do not claim FORGE is operationally 24×7 merely because its code exists or a workflow is scheduled.

A 24×7 operational claim requires evidence of:

- Deployed persistent controller.
- Durable task queue.
- Cloud worker execution.
- Actual repository access.
- Actual permitted coding-agent execution.
- Monitoring and alert delivery.
- Restart and recovery behavior.
- Resource and cost controls.
- Durable task and evidence persistence.

Report unavailable capabilities honestly.

